  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nEarly afternoon analysis showed large scale upper level \ntroughing across the eastern CONUS with a ridge axis basically \nover the Rockies. This left us under northwest flow aloft with \nsurface ridging contributing to quiet and cool weather. The \nsurface ridging will remain in place overnight with winds \nremaining light, so it's setting up for our coldest night of the\nseason thus far, with lows in the lower to mid 20s. That said, \nsome short term guidance hints at some lingering clouds \novernight, especially near and east of the Missouri River. If \nthose end up being a little more expansive than currently \nthought, lows Friday morning may end up several degrees higher \nthan forecast, but still almost certainly sub-freezing (95% \nchance). Friday into Friday night will be similar to today and \ntonight, with highs in the 40s, lows in the lower to mid 20s, \nand winds remaining light as the surface high passes by to our \neast.\n\nWe'll start a slow, but steady warming trend Saturday as the \nridge to our west starts to advance eastward and southerly low \nlevel flow builds in. Expect mid to upper 40s for the weekend \nbefore some 50s start to return by Monday and Tuesday, and a few\nspots near 60 by Wednesday.\n\nDry weather will be favored through at least Tuesday with \nguidance now in good agreement that a shortwave trough and \nassociated precip currently off the Pacific Northwest shore will\nstay to our southwest. The next chances arrive on Wednesday as \na surface low passes by to the north and drags a cold front \nthrough the area. Still quite a bit of spread on exacting timing\nand track, and latest trends have been toward a drier frontal \npassage, but chances remain in the 20-40% range Wednesday into \nearly Thursday. Looking ahead, guidance is in decent agreement \nof another system moving through at some point during the \nweekend, but being 9-10 days out, overall forecast confidence is\npretty low.",
